WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.090 --> 00:00:02.830
- If I were to ask you
why you originally created

00:00:02.830 --> 00:00:04.480
your social media accounts,

00:00:04.480 --> 00:00:06.680
you might come up with
something similar to,

00:00:06.680 --> 00:00:08.870
well, to keep up with friends and family

00:00:08.870 --> 00:00:11.260
and see what they're
doing and stuff like that.

00:00:11.260 --> 00:00:12.990
And that's not a bad reason at all.

00:00:12.990 --> 00:00:15.620
In fact, it's quite an honorable sentiment

00:00:15.620 --> 00:00:19.370
but as society becomes more
dependent on these technologies,

00:00:19.370 --> 00:00:21.010
there's an ever-growing problem

00:00:21.010 --> 00:00:22.980
and that is that our intended use

00:00:22.980 --> 00:00:24.960
for these social media applications

00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:27.530
and what our daily use actually looks like

00:00:27.530 --> 00:00:29.870
are often two completely different things.

00:00:29.870 --> 00:00:31.660
Many of us use social media

00:00:31.660 --> 00:00:34.150
and our smartphones compulsively,

00:00:34.150 --> 00:00:35.750
it's like a nervous tick.

00:00:35.750 --> 00:00:38.600
There's a certain need to
check your phone all the time.

00:00:38.600 --> 00:00:41.320
It's constantly pulling
your attention away

00:00:41.320 --> 00:00:42.940
from whatever's in front of you

00:00:42.940 --> 00:00:45.570
and back into your other online life.

00:00:45.570 --> 00:00:47.670
And I'm not gonna say
this is ruining the world

00:00:47.670 --> 00:00:48.950
or anything like that,

00:00:48.950 --> 00:00:50.938
people are still showing
up to work on time

00:00:50.938 --> 00:00:55.270
and countries are still being
run decently well I guess

00:00:55.270 --> 00:00:57.960
but the effects of this underlying itch

00:00:57.960 --> 00:01:01.210
is still measurable and it's
felt by pretty much anyone

00:01:01.210 --> 00:01:02.500
who owns a smartphone.

00:01:02.500 --> 00:01:04.220
Maybe it's when you're mid conversation

00:01:04.220 --> 00:01:05.760
with somebody and things are going well

00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:07.150
and you're having a good conversation.

00:01:07.150 --> 00:01:08.760
And then that itch starts to happen

00:01:08.760 --> 00:01:11.040
and you just wanna pull out
your phone for no reason

00:01:11.040 --> 00:01:13.460
even if you're just swiping between pages,

00:01:13.460 --> 00:01:14.810
you're not really paying attention

00:01:14.810 --> 00:01:16.360
to what the person is saying.

00:01:16.360 --> 00:01:18.840
Or maybe it's when you're
trying to write an email

00:01:18.840 --> 00:01:21.920
or an essay or an article
and it's a little bit boring

00:01:21.920 --> 00:01:25.150
and you find yourself drifting
towards the new tab button

00:01:25.150 --> 00:01:27.940
just to hit F, Enter and realize

00:01:27.940 --> 00:01:30.620
that you already checked
Facebook like 45 seconds ago

00:01:30.620 --> 00:01:32.470
and there were no notifications

00:01:32.470 --> 00:01:34.160
but you still checked anyways.

00:01:34.160 --> 00:01:35.930
And that's exactly what I'm talking about.

00:01:35.930 --> 00:01:37.750
And the weird thing is this whole thing

00:01:37.750 --> 00:01:39.730
is an entirely new struggle.

00:01:39.730 --> 00:01:43.190
Humanity has only been dealing
with it for about nine years.

00:01:43.190 --> 00:01:46.630
Apple rolled out the
original iPhone in 2007

00:01:46.630 --> 00:01:49.120
but it didn't launch with
any social media apps

00:01:49.120 --> 00:01:50.540
and it didn't even have an app store.

00:01:50.540 --> 00:01:55.020
Facebook didn't reach 500
million users until July 2010,

00:01:55.020 --> 00:01:58.640
a number which is now
2.3 billion and Instagram

00:01:58.640 --> 00:02:01.500
wasn't even invented
until later that year.

00:02:01.500 --> 00:02:04.620
So the relationship between
humanity and social media

00:02:04.620 --> 00:02:06.500
is still in its infancy.

00:02:06.500 --> 00:02:08.410
And we're just kind of stumbling around,

00:02:08.410 --> 00:02:10.870
hitting our heads on
walls, trying to figure out

00:02:10.870 --> 00:02:13.710
the best way to reap the
rewards and the usefulness

00:02:13.710 --> 00:02:15.780
of these apps without hurting ourselves

00:02:15.780 --> 00:02:17.840
and our well-being in the process.

00:02:17.840 --> 00:02:19.490
So if you are somebody who looks

00:02:19.490 --> 00:02:22.650
at their phone way more often
than you would like to admit,

00:02:22.650 --> 00:02:24.410
I've definitely been guilty of this,

00:02:24.410 --> 00:02:25.890
there's actually evidence to believe

00:02:25.890 --> 00:02:27.210
that it's not your fault.

00:02:27.210 --> 00:02:29.240
Because most of these apps are designed

00:02:29.240 --> 00:02:31.500
from the ground up to be addictive,

00:02:31.500 --> 00:02:35.010
behind the glimmering promise
of increased human connection

00:02:35.010 --> 00:02:37.610
is basically a slot machine in your pocket

00:02:37.610 --> 00:02:40.020
where you pull a lever
and fun stuff comes out.

00:02:40.020 --> 00:02:42.710
And these rewards are
some of the most powerful

00:02:42.710 --> 00:02:45.180
and ancient rewards that can be exploited

00:02:45.180 --> 00:02:47.780
in the human brain, social validation

00:02:47.780 --> 00:02:49.140
and I'm not making all this up,

00:02:49.140 --> 00:02:50.580
this isn't some giant conspiracy.

00:02:50.580 --> 00:02:53.790
This is literally how
these apps make money.

00:02:53.790 --> 00:02:57.370
They get paid by third-party
companies who purchase ad space

00:02:57.370 --> 00:02:59.790
on their platform in the hopes that you,

00:02:59.790 --> 00:03:01.410
the user interacts with it.

00:03:01.410 --> 00:03:03.380
So the more time you spend on these apps,

00:03:03.380 --> 00:03:06.480
the more likely you are
to click on or engage

00:03:06.480 --> 00:03:09.240
with these ads and the
more money these companies

00:03:09.240 --> 00:03:11.020
and these platforms make.

00:03:11.020 --> 00:03:14.610
These multi-billion dollar
companies are built entirely

00:03:14.610 --> 00:03:16.752
by taking control not of your money,

00:03:16.752 --> 00:03:20.690
not of your land or your
livestock but of your attention

00:03:20.690 --> 00:03:23.030
because your attention is their currency.

00:03:23.030 --> 00:03:26.060
And that's the underlying issue
is we feel kind of helpless

00:03:26.060 --> 00:03:29.350
to compulsively use these apps
but they're kind of useful

00:03:29.350 --> 00:03:30.710
so you sort of need them,

00:03:30.710 --> 00:03:32.900
they do enhance your life in some ways

00:03:32.900 --> 00:03:35.020
and you do use them for useful things

00:03:35.020 --> 00:03:37.440
but they're taking away
from your life in other ways

00:03:37.440 --> 00:03:39.000
because of their addictive nature.

00:03:39.000 --> 00:03:41.730
Thankfully though, if you
watch this channel at all,

00:03:41.730 --> 00:03:44.130
I don't make videos about
why we're all screwed

00:03:44.130 --> 00:03:45.620
and can't do anything about it

00:03:45.620 --> 00:03:49.000
because that's definitely not
true, you can find a balance,

00:03:49.000 --> 00:03:51.880
you can maximize the
benefits of social media

00:03:51.880 --> 00:03:54.300
while not falling prey
to it's addictive effects

00:03:54.300 --> 00:03:57.460
but unfortunately you can't
attain this sort of freedom

00:03:57.460 --> 00:03:58.742
by little quick tips and tricks.

00:03:58.742 --> 00:04:02.979
Don't use your phone as an alarm clock,

00:04:02.979 --> 00:04:06.250
only use your phone for
one hour in the day.

00:04:06.250 --> 00:04:08.800
You really do need a paradigm shift.

00:04:08.800 --> 00:04:12.550
You need to adopt a new
philosophy towards technology use.

00:04:12.550 --> 00:04:14.470
One philosophy in particular

00:04:14.470 --> 00:04:17.130
is leading a sort of silent revolution

00:04:17.130 --> 00:04:19.320
and it's helping people regain their focus

00:04:19.320 --> 00:04:21.600
and take back control over their lives,

00:04:21.600 --> 00:04:23.870
it's called digital minimalism.

00:04:23.870 --> 00:04:25.750
In his book by the same name,

00:04:25.750 --> 00:04:28.857
Cal Newport defines digital minimalism as,

00:04:28.857 --> 00:04:32.007
"A philosophy of technology
use in which you focus

00:04:32.007 --> 00:04:35.717
"your online time on a small
number of carefully selected

00:04:35.717 --> 00:04:39.197
"and optimized activities
that strongly support

00:04:39.197 --> 00:04:42.127
"the things you value
and then happily miss out

00:04:42.127 --> 00:04:43.440
"on everything else."

00:04:43.440 --> 00:04:45.440
So to better understand digital minimalism

00:04:45.440 --> 00:04:47.910
and how you can apply it
to your life right now,

00:04:47.910 --> 00:04:49.380
one thing that was useful to me

00:04:49.380 --> 00:04:51.960
in understanding how
digital minimalism works

00:04:51.960 --> 00:04:54.950
is to compare the philosophies
of digital minimalism

00:04:54.950 --> 00:04:56.680
and digital maximalism, okay.

00:04:56.680 --> 00:04:59.520
So the digital minimalist
doesn't mind missing out

00:04:59.520 --> 00:05:01.390
on a potential small benefit

00:05:01.390 --> 00:05:03.400
that a certain technology might give them

00:05:03.400 --> 00:05:06.770
if it has any risk of
decreasing the quality of things

00:05:06.770 --> 00:05:09.900
that they already know to be
of tremendous value to them,

00:05:09.900 --> 00:05:11.640
like their ability to focus,

00:05:11.640 --> 00:05:15.324
contrast this with the philosophy
of the digital maximalist

00:05:15.324 --> 00:05:18.920
who believes that any
small potential benefit

00:05:18.920 --> 00:05:20.490
is worth downloading the app.

00:05:20.490 --> 00:05:21.920
If they think that Instagram

00:05:21.920 --> 00:05:23.100
will be a little bit fun for them

00:05:23.100 --> 00:05:25.810
or they have any sort of
impulsive reason to download it,

00:05:25.810 --> 00:05:27.430
they downloaded it immediately.

00:05:27.430 --> 00:05:30.060
If some application claims
to make you more productive

00:05:30.060 --> 00:05:32.530
or more connected or anything,
they'll just download it

00:05:32.530 --> 00:05:34.970
without considering the larger picture

00:05:34.970 --> 00:05:38.220
of how that applications daily
use will fit into their lives

00:05:38.220 --> 00:05:39.640
and whether they really need it.

00:05:39.640 --> 00:05:42.060
So we've talked about the
theory of digital minimalism

00:05:42.060 --> 00:05:44.570
but I wanna leave you guys
with some practical wisdom

00:05:44.570 --> 00:05:47.680
as to how you can apply
this directly right now

00:05:47.680 --> 00:05:49.890
in your lives so the
number one thing you can do

00:05:49.890 --> 00:05:51.580
is a two-fold process.

00:05:51.580 --> 00:05:54.860
The first one is to vet
every application you use

00:05:54.860 --> 00:05:56.810
and you do this by asking two questions.

00:05:56.810 --> 00:05:58.530
Number one is what are the benefits

00:05:58.530 --> 00:05:59.920
of using this application?

00:05:59.920 --> 00:06:02.200
And number two is, is this the best way

00:06:02.200 --> 00:06:03.650
to achieve these benefits?

00:06:03.650 --> 00:06:05.780
So let's take Instagram again for example,

00:06:05.780 --> 00:06:07.010
I like to rip on Instagram

00:06:07.010 --> 00:06:08.730
because I actually deleted it right

00:06:08.730 --> 00:06:10.490
after reading digital minimalism

00:06:10.490 --> 00:06:11.940
so when I was deciding to delete it,

00:06:11.940 --> 00:06:14.180
my thought process went like this.

00:06:14.180 --> 00:06:16.650
What are the benefits of
using this application

00:06:16.650 --> 00:06:18.420
and my perceived benefits
that I was getting

00:06:18.420 --> 00:06:20.710
from Instagram probably was two-fold.

00:06:20.710 --> 00:06:23.840
First one was like, I like
to keep up with friends

00:06:23.840 --> 00:06:25.670
and family and see what they're up to.

00:06:25.670 --> 00:06:28.280
And by seeing updates on baby pictures

00:06:28.280 --> 00:06:29.790
and engagements and stuff like that,

00:06:29.790 --> 00:06:32.300
I felt more connected
to my friends network

00:06:32.300 --> 00:06:34.450
and the second benefit I
experienced from Instagram

00:06:34.450 --> 00:06:36.870
was probably that it was just fun,

00:06:36.870 --> 00:06:38.720
I guess it was mildly entertaining

00:06:38.720 --> 00:06:40.030
when I wanted to break from something

00:06:40.030 --> 00:06:41.510
I could check Instagram and it gave me

00:06:41.510 --> 00:06:42.510
a little dopamine hit.

00:06:42.510 --> 00:06:44.240
So then I ask the second question,

00:06:44.240 --> 00:06:47.020
is this the best way to
achieve those benefits?

00:06:47.020 --> 00:06:50.080
And no, they weren't the best
way to achieve those benefits.

00:06:50.080 --> 00:06:53.290
If staying up to date with my
friends is important to me,

00:06:53.290 --> 00:06:54.930
then the best way to achieve that benefit

00:06:54.930 --> 00:06:58.390
is to call them or text
them to set up a time

00:06:58.390 --> 00:07:00.870
to actually connect with
them on a profound level.

00:07:00.870 --> 00:07:03.520
Maybe not even a profound
level, just a genuine level

00:07:03.520 --> 00:07:06.550
because Instagram is super
superficial, super superficial.

00:07:06.550 --> 00:07:09.200
You're not actually exchanging
ideas and information

00:07:09.200 --> 00:07:10.810
and getting to know the person any better.

00:07:10.810 --> 00:07:13.930
So in a way, Instagram wasn't offering me

00:07:13.930 --> 00:07:16.640
that perceived benefit that
I thought I was getting

00:07:16.640 --> 00:07:17.940
from it to begin with.

00:07:17.940 --> 00:07:19.840
Secondly, there are way better ways

00:07:19.840 --> 00:07:22.269
that I can entertain myself
that doesn't completely

00:07:22.269 --> 00:07:23.960
waste my time.

00:07:23.960 --> 00:07:26.650
If I want a dopamine hit,
maybe I should go work out.

00:07:26.650 --> 00:07:29.650
There are healthier ways that
I can get this entertainment.

00:07:29.650 --> 00:07:32.610
So for that reason, Instagram
didn't really offer me

00:07:32.610 --> 00:07:35.880
much value that I couldn't
get in other areas of my life.

00:07:35.880 --> 00:07:36.713
So I deleted it.

00:07:36.713 --> 00:07:39.270
And since I deleted it
that's one less thing

00:07:39.270 --> 00:07:41.850
that's pulling me away and
sending me push notifications

00:07:41.850 --> 00:07:45.210
and tempting me to go waste my time on it

00:07:45.210 --> 00:07:47.630
that way I can use that gained time

00:07:47.630 --> 00:07:49.540
and redirected towards the things

00:07:49.540 --> 00:07:50.960
that are important in my life.

00:07:50.960 --> 00:07:53.610
And that's what digital
minimalism is at its core,

00:07:53.610 --> 00:07:57.160
it's being intentional
with why you use your apps

00:07:57.160 --> 00:07:59.010
that are on your phone, on your computer

00:07:59.010 --> 00:08:01.310
and figuring out why
they exist in your life.

00:08:01.310 --> 00:08:03.780
For instance, I also
deleted LinkedIn recently

00:08:03.780 --> 00:08:06.270
because my perceived
benefit of having LinkedIn

00:08:06.270 --> 00:08:08.820
was that I was more
available to collaborate

00:08:08.820 --> 00:08:10.590
with in my professional network.

00:08:10.590 --> 00:08:13.180
I have only ever gotten
one job opportunity

00:08:13.180 --> 00:08:16.610
from LinkedIn earlier on in
my freelance video career

00:08:16.610 --> 00:08:18.980
and that's it on a day-to-day basis,

00:08:18.980 --> 00:08:21.330
all it's doing is sending
me push notifications

00:08:21.330 --> 00:08:22.960
and emails and stuff like that,

00:08:22.960 --> 00:08:25.260
telling me that people joined my network

00:08:25.260 --> 00:08:26.900
and I'm like, oh great, in my mind,

00:08:26.900 --> 00:08:29.090
I'm like this is more business
opportunity potentially.

00:08:29.090 --> 00:08:31.570
I'm a well-connected
guy, it's not worth it.

00:08:31.570 --> 00:08:33.820
It offers zero value

00:08:33.820 --> 00:08:35.980
but since I used to be
a digital maximalist,

00:08:35.980 --> 00:08:38.650
I'm like, oh, there could
be some potential value

00:08:38.650 --> 00:08:40.980
to having this app and that's just done.

00:08:40.980 --> 00:08:43.720
So the second way that you
can apply digital minimalism

00:08:43.720 --> 00:08:46.770
to your life right now is to optimize.

00:08:46.770 --> 00:08:49.630
Digital minimalism isn't all
about just deleting every app

00:08:49.630 --> 00:08:51.790
and then just facing the
consequences of the ones

00:08:51.790 --> 00:08:52.623
you don't delete.

00:08:52.623 --> 00:08:54.190
Part of it is optimization.

00:08:54.190 --> 00:08:57.280
So for instance, I can't
really get away from Facebook.

00:08:57.280 --> 00:09:00.120
A good example of this
is my Facebook group

00:09:00.120 --> 00:09:01.460
for this YouTube channel,

00:09:01.460 --> 00:09:03.970
it's doing very well, it's
very helpful for people,

00:09:03.970 --> 00:09:07.630
it has over 3,000 members and
that sort of accessibility

00:09:07.630 --> 00:09:10.980
for a group isn't really
available on any other platform.

00:09:10.980 --> 00:09:12.760
So I know this whole Facebook group thing

00:09:12.760 --> 00:09:15.540
is a useful thing for my YouTube channel.

00:09:15.540 --> 00:09:18.490
So how do I use Facebook
and not get distracted by it

00:09:18.490 --> 00:09:20.480
and that's where optimization comes in.

00:09:20.480 --> 00:09:23.220
The main thing about Facebook
that pretty much anyone

00:09:23.220 --> 00:09:25.890
would find distracting is the newsfeed.

00:09:25.890 --> 00:09:28.130
One thing that I did was I installed

00:09:28.130 --> 00:09:31.410
a Newsfeed Eradicator, it's
available for Firefox, Chrome,

00:09:31.410 --> 00:09:33.100
I'm pretty sure Safari as well.

00:09:33.100 --> 00:09:36.670
And that replaces the
home page, the newsfeed

00:09:36.670 --> 00:09:38.350
with a motivational quote.

00:09:38.350 --> 00:09:39.470
I don't really read the quote

00:09:39.470 --> 00:09:42.268
but just like it just
gets rid of the newsfeed

00:09:42.268 --> 00:09:43.101
and that's super helpful.

00:09:43.101 --> 00:09:45.630
And the second thing I did
was I deleted the Facebook app

00:09:45.630 --> 00:09:48.430
from my phone because that
also has all these options

00:09:48.430 --> 00:09:51.350
for watching videos and playing games

00:09:51.350 --> 00:09:53.930
and stuff like that at the
bottom, I don't need that crap.

00:09:53.930 --> 00:09:55.840
I basically only need the messenger

00:09:55.840 --> 00:09:57.960
and then when I really want to check in

00:09:57.960 --> 00:10:00.373
and allow people into the Facebook group,

00:10:00.373 --> 00:10:02.920
I'll physically type
in Facebook and do that

00:10:02.920 --> 00:10:05.490
and that's it, that's my
entire Facebook experience.

00:10:05.490 --> 00:10:08.280
It's a really stripped down
and it allows me to focus

00:10:08.280 --> 00:10:11.490
and it has allowed me to
view Facebook more as a tool

00:10:11.490 --> 00:10:14.440
for my business rather than a excuse

00:10:14.440 --> 00:10:16.580
to waste like 30 minutes watching

00:10:16.580 --> 00:10:18.080
these viral Facebook videos.

00:10:18.080 --> 00:10:19.450
So those are just a few ways

00:10:19.450 --> 00:10:21.170
that you can apply digital minimalism

00:10:21.170 --> 00:10:23.750
to your life right now, it's a dense book,

00:10:23.750 --> 00:10:25.550
it's so rich with great knowledge

00:10:25.550 --> 00:10:27.880
and it is probably one of
the best books I've read

00:10:27.880 --> 00:10:31.000
in a long time in terms
of changing my perspective

00:10:31.000 --> 00:10:33.230
and I think it's a must read for anybody

00:10:33.230 --> 00:10:35.830
who uses technology on a daily basis

00:10:35.830 --> 00:10:37.930
especially for people who are struggling

00:10:37.930 --> 00:10:40.020
to pull themselves away from their phone.

00:10:40.020 --> 00:10:41.210
I'll put a link in the description

00:10:41.210 --> 00:10:43.520
where you can pick up
your copy of the book.

00:10:43.520 --> 00:10:44.830
Couldn't recommend it enough.

00:10:44.830 --> 00:10:46.470
If you like this video, make sure to hit

00:10:46.470 --> 00:10:48.660
that like button because when you like it,

00:10:48.660 --> 00:10:50.614
YouTube recommends it to more people,

00:10:50.614 --> 00:10:53.490
YouTube algorithm sees it as a good video

00:10:53.490 --> 00:10:56.310
and I think this topic in
particular is really important

00:10:56.310 --> 00:10:58.780
for people to see in today's day and age.

00:10:58.780 --> 00:11:01.110
If you're lurking here,
consider subscribing

00:11:01.110 --> 00:11:04.370
because I make videos like
this on a regular basis

00:11:04.370 --> 00:11:05.870
and hopefully I can help you out

